---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import PersonHouseholdRecords from '../../components/PersonHouseholdRecords.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'base-registry')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Maintain the records your public services rely on."
  description="Base Registry Engine maintains the records a government institution is responsible for, such as people, businesses, licences, places, or assets. It applies the institution’s access rules, keeps a history of changes, and lets approved staff applications and online services work with the register."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="registry-lifecycle-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">One example: a population register</p>
        <h2 id="registry-lifecycle-title">Maintain linked records as circumstances change.</h2>
        <p>A population registry office could use the engine for a register of residents and households. Staff register people, find existing records, and update details as circumstances change. The engine stores the information and its history; your application provides the screens.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="record-example" aria-labelledby="record-example-caption">
        <div class="stored-records">
          <p class="record-owner">Stored in Base Registry Engine</p>
          <PersonHouseholdRecords />
          <p class="record-storage">Two linked records, each with its own fields and change history.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="record-lifecycle">
          <li><h3>Register and connect</h3><p>Create a person’s record and link it to a household. The engine checks required information, permitted values, and references before saving it.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Find and use</h3><p>Look up a resident or retrieve the people linked to a household. Each application can access the records and fields its permissions allow.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Keep up with changes</h3><p>Correct a detail or update a household relationship. The engine stores the current information and preserves earlier revisions so authorized users can follow what changed.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption id="record-example-caption">Illustrative register with fictional records. The same approach can link a business to its establishments, a professional to licences, or an asset to its location.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-records-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practical capabilities</p>
        <h2 id="breg-records-title">Manage records under the same rules across your services.</h2>
        <p>Your institution defines the information, relationships, and permissions once. Staff applications, online services, and connected systems then work with the register under those rules.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Define connected records</h3><p>Set the kinds of records the register holds, their required information, valid values, and relationships. A register can connect people to households, businesses to establishments, or professionals to licences.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Control who may read and change them</h3><p>Give each staff role or connected service access to the records and fields it needs. Where staff approval is required, connect a review service such as Registry Casework. The register applies approved changes under your institution’s rules.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Follow each record over time</h3><p>Keep earlier revisions so authorized staff can see what changed, and prevent an older edit from overwriting a newer one. Changes to the register’s structure or rules use reviewed configuration and, where needed, a planned data migration.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="fit" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="breg-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your organization</p>
        <h2 id="breg-fit-title">Build a register that works with your existing services.</h2>
        <p>Base Registry Engine maintains records behind staff applications and online services. Use it to build a new register or move existing records into a defined structure. An implementation combines the engine with the screens, identity service, and system connections the service needs.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your institution defines the register</dt><dd>Agree what each record means, how records relate, which changes are allowed, and who may read or change the information.</dd>
        <dt>Choose who delivers the implementation</dt><dd>Aubex, another delivery partner, or your own team can configure the engine, provide the required screens, connect sign-in and other systems, and plan data migration. A written scope should agree hosting, operation, support, and handover.</dd>
        <dt>Your operators run the register</dt><dd>Run the engine and its database on infrastructure your organization controls, with the backups, monitoring, access management, and change procedures your service requires.</dd>
        <dt>Add information checks and staff reviews</dt><dd>Connect <a href="/products/evidence-gateway/">Evidence Gateway</a> when another service needs to check specific information, or <a href="/products/casework/">Registry Casework</a> when staff must review a request and record a decision.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="breg-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="breg-developers-title">Build and inspect a small register.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial creates a local contact register, adds and reads one contact, and checks that another caller cannot list it. Continue with the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/configuration-defined-registry/">configuration explanation</a> and <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/operate/breg/">operations documentation</a> when planning a real implementation.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>{product.tutorialLabel} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>

<style>
  .record-example { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 24px 40px; align-items: start; margin: 0; }
  .stored-records { min-width: 0; background: var(--registry-slate-tint); border: 1px solid var(--registry-slate-rule); padding: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px); }
  .record-owner { color: var(--registry-blue); font-size: var(--type-sm); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .record-storage { color: var(--registry-body); font-size: var(--type-xs); line-height: 1.6; margin: 16px 0 0; }
  .record-lifecycle { display: grid; gap: 22px; margin: 0; padding-left: 26px; }
  .record-lifecycle li { padding-left: 10px; }
  .record-lifecycle li::marker { color: var(--registry-blue); font-family: var(--registry-mono); font-weight: 600; }
  .record-lifecycle h3 { font-size: var(--type-subhead); margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .record-lifecycle p { margin: 0; }
  .record-example figcaption { grid-column: 1 / -1; color: var(--registry-muted); font-size: var(--type-base); line-height: 1.5; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .record-example { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
